from aspen import Response
from gratipay.utils import markdown
[---]
if user.ANON:       raise Response(401)
if not user.ADMIN:  raise Response(403)
request.allow('GET')

banner = _("API")
title = _("Chart of Accounts")

listing = {"accounts": website.db.all('SELECT * FROM accounts ORDER BY number ASC')}
[---] application/json via json_dump
listing
[---] text/html
{% extends "templates/api-accounts.html" %}
{% block content %}{{ markdown.render("""

Gratipay's chart of accounts is in the `accounts` database table. It's a simple
mapping of account `number` to `name`, and forms the basis for our general
ledger. Our convention is to encode the type of account in the account number
like so:

 - **1xx**&mdash;Assets
 - **2xx**&mdash;Liabilities
 - **3xx**&mdash;Income
 - **4xx**&mdash;Expenses

Every ~user and Team on Gratipay has an account in the chart of accounts, as
well as every bank and payments partner that we've used.


## Role Required

`admin`


## GET

Returns a listing of accounts from the chart of accounts, sorted by `number`
ascending. Gotcha: account numbers are text and are sorted as such.

```
$ curl -H \"Accept: application/json\" -u $user_id:$api_key https://gratipay.com/api/v2/accounts/
[{\"number\": \"101\": \"name\": \"Accounts Receivable\"},
 {\"number\": \"201\": \"name\": \"Accounts Payable\"}]
```

## Drill-Down

[`/api/v2/accounts/%number`](/api/v2/accounts/number)

""") }}
{% endblock %}
